<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">All Blacks</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1884">1884</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as New Zealand national rugby union team</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The All Blacks are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>'s national rugby union team, named for their black kit and known for performing the haka before matches. Since their first international tour in 1884 they have become one of the most successful teams in any sport, winning the Rugby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifa-world-cup">World Cup</a> in 1987, 2011 and 2015. Rugby and the team occupy a central place in New Zealand's national identity.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In culture and sport, the mountaineer Sir Edmund Hillary, the <b>All Blacks</b> rugby team and the films of Peter Jackson made the country widely known, and Jacinda Ardern's response to the Christchurch mosque shootings of 2019 drew worldwide attention.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…Mate&quot; became the best-known haka in New Zealand and abroad because a choreographed, synchronised version has been performed before test matches by the <b>All Blacks</b>, New Zealand's rugby union team, and by the Kiwis, its rugby league team, until 2013, when the Kiwis took up &quot;Te Iwi Kiwi&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ka-mate">Ka Mate</a></li><li><blockquote>…2017 the New Zealand Rugby Union, in a campaign led by the police constable and former All Black Glen Osborne, urged <b>All Blacks</b> fans to adopt the song as a rallying chant to out-sing the supporters of the British and Irish Lions during their tour of New Zealand.</blockquote><a href="/songs/tutira-mai-nga-iwi">Tūtira Mai Ngā Iwi</a></li></ul></section>
